---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const title = 'Trustee Duties and Responsibilities | FreeTrustDocs';
const description = 'Trustee fiduciary duties explained: duty of loyalty, prudence, impartiality, accounting, and what happens when duties are breached.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogType="article">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>›</span> <a href="/guide/">Guides</a> <span>›</span> Trustee Duties and Responsibilities
      </div>
      <h1>Trustee Duties and Responsibilities</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A trustee is a fiduciary — legally bound to act in the beneficiaries' best interests. Understanding
        these duties is essential whether you're naming a trustee or serving as one.
      </p>

      <h2>The Core Fiduciary Duties</h2>

      <h3>1. Duty of Loyalty</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must act solely in the beneficiaries' interests. They cannot use trust assets for personal
        benefit, accept undisclosed compensation, or engage in self-dealing. Any conflict of interest must be
        disclosed. A trustee who profits from their position must account for and return those profits.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Duty of Prudence (Prudent Investor Rule)</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must invest and manage trust assets as a prudent person would. Most states follow the
        Uniform Prudent Investor Act, which requires:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Considering the trust's purposes, distribution requirements, and tax implications</li>
        <li>Diversifying investments (unless the trustee reasonably determines diversification would be detrimental)</li>
        <li>Evaluating investments as part of the overall portfolio, not individually</li>
        <li>Considering both risk and return</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Duty of Impartiality</h3>
      <p>
        When there are multiple beneficiaries, the trustee must treat them fairly according to the trust terms.
        This doesn't mean equal treatment — it means following the trust's instructions without favoritism.
        Income beneficiaries and remainder beneficiaries often have competing interests, and the trustee must
        balance them.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Duty to Inform and Account</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must keep beneficiaries informed about trust administration. This includes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Providing an annual accounting of trust assets, income, expenses, and distributions</li>
        <li>Notifying beneficiaries of the trust's existence and their interests</li>
        <li>Responding to reasonable requests for information about the trust</li>
        <li>Providing copies of the trust agreement when required by state law</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5. Duty to Preserve and Protect Trust Property</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must take reasonable steps to protect trust assets — maintaining insurance, securing property,
        collecting income, and addressing risks. Negligence in protecting trust property can result in personal
        liability.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Duty Not to Delegate (with exceptions)</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must personally perform their duties. However, they can delegate functions that require
        specialized expertise (investment management, tax preparation, legal work) — provided they exercise
        reasonable care in selecting and monitoring the delegate.
      </p>

      <h3>7. Duty to Enforce and Defend Claims</h3>
      <p>
        The trustee must take reasonable steps to enforce claims on behalf of the trust and defend claims against
        the trust. Sitting on a valid claim or failing to defend a lawsuit can result in liability.
      </p>

      <h2>What Happens When a Trustee Breaches Their Duties?</h2>
      <p>
        A breach of fiduciary duty can result in:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Personal liability</strong> — the trustee may have to repay losses from their own funds</li>
        <li><strong>Removal</strong> — a court can remove the trustee and appoint a successor</li>
        <li><strong>Surcharge</strong> — the trustee may be ordered to pay damages or forfeit fees</li>
        <li><strong>Criminal charges</strong> — in cases of fraud or embezzlement</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Beneficiaries can petition the court to compel an accounting, surcharge the trustee, or remove them.
        Most states have statutes of limitations on trust claims — typically 1-3 years from when the beneficiary
        discovered (or should have discovered) the breach.
      </p>

      <h2>Practical Tips for Trustees</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep meticulous records of every transaction</li>
        <li>Never commingle trust assets with personal assets</li>
        <li>Document all decisions and the reasoning behind them</li>
        <li>Communicate regularly with beneficiaries</li>
        <li>Get professional help when needed (accountant, attorney, investment advisor)</li>
        <li>Review the trust agreement carefully — it governs everything</li>
      </ul>

      <Callout variant="tip" label="Choosing a trustee">
        <p>
          These duties are why choosing the right trustee matters so much.
          <a href="/guide/how-to-choose-a-trustee/">See our trustee selection guide →</a>
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <a href="/create/declaration-of-trust/" class="btn btn-primary">Create a Declaration of Trust →</a>
      </div>

      <div class="upl-notice" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <strong>Not legal advice.</strong> This article is for educational purposes only. Trust laws vary by state.
        Consult a licensed attorney for guidance on trustee duties and liabilities in your jurisdiction.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>